WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.760
Recently, more than a couple of knitters

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:07.140
contacted me to tell me they just
couldn't get their gauge right.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.060
One had knit a sleeve
and it was way too long.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:14.460
The other one had knit an entire
sweater and it was way too big.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.880
No, there isn't anything
wrong with the pattern.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.180
It's just math.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.560
Let's go back to the swatch
and see where they're struggling.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.500
I often ask knitters to take
a photo of their swatch with rulers.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:34.140
In a it doesn't matter if you use
machine netter scale or a regular ruler.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:40.000
Often having another set of eyes to look
at your swatch reveals the problem.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.420
This knitter insisted
that her gauge was 18.5 stitches to 4 inches.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.880
Now take a look and can you see that she

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.980
had positioned her ruler scale
along the edge of the knitting?

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:59.400
The yellow ruler is designed
to measure over 30 stitches.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.100
Back to the drawing board,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:07.200
she measured between 30 stitches
and got an accurate gauge.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.980
In this example, can you see the problem?

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:18.360
Using the stitch scale to measure
rows will cause all sorts of problems.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.720
Some time ago, a dear friend was sad

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.180
because she had run out
of yarn for her project.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.440
She couldn't get any more of it.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.850
She showed me her swatch,
and I was She was amazed.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.020
She told me the ball band said
that the gauge was three stitches per inch,

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:40.380
so she cast on three stitches
and it measured one inch.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.640
What was wrong with that?

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.120
Obviously, she needed
to knit a larger swatch.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.930
This last example has
a whole bunch of problems.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.140
First, it isn't big enough.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.480
We need to knit a swatch that is large

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.640
enough to measure a four-inch
square in the middle.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.120
We can measure gauge more accurately

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.800
without those curled
edges impacting things.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.100
Next, it looks like the knitter wants
to use the yellow scale,

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:17.450
but she needs 30 stitches in the middle
of her knitting to use the scale.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.920
She didn't make a large enough swatch.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.380
Third, she marked some stitches with pins.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:29.260
But why take time to count
stitches and do all that math?

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:33.320
Mark them as you knit
and there's no counting.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.760
Next, the curled edges are going to make

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.500
it much more difficult
to measure accurately.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.200
Dress your swatch, wash,
and block it as you're going to treat your

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:46.580
project so that you
can accurately measure.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.120
It makes a big difference.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.140
Lastly, this bind-off
edge is way too tight.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.100
It's distorting the edge,

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:01.040
and the edge is making it impossible
to measure the gauge accurately.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.260
I don't like to use the word worthless,
but in this case,

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.120
this knitter needs to start all over
and knit a proper swatch that's large

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:15.500
enough to mark stitches and rows
according to the scale she'll use.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:21.060
She needs to bind off loosely
and dress her swatch.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.480
She'll have much more success
with gauge in her knitting.

